Reasoning: MemFree collapsing exactly during a backup window smells like page cache, not application demand — backup reads fill the cache by design. Check what the alert actually measures.
<|call:check_host_dashboard|> {"host": "web-prod-3", "panel": "memory"}
System Response: [RED] mem_used_pct 98.1% (total 64.3G minus free 1.2G). Climb began 01:04, when backup started; app RSS panel flat at 19.8G all night.
Recovery reasoning: The panel computes used as total-minus-free, which counts page cache as consumption — and the climb tracks the backup reads while app RSS never moved. That is the tell it is cache, not demand. MemAvailable predicts OOM risk; read the kernel's numbers.
<|call:read_meminfo|> {"host": "web-prod-3", "fields": ["MemFree", "MemAvailable", "Cached", "Dirty"]}
System Response: MemFree 1233408 kB, MemAvailable 42011648 kB, Cached 40894464 kB, Dirty 18432 kB.
Recovery reasoning: MemAvailable is 42G of 64G — about 40G of clean cache is reclaimable on demand, and Dirty is only 18M. Confirm nothing is actually stalling on memory.
<|call:psi_read|> {"host": "web-prod-3", "resource": "memory"}
System Response: some avg10=0.00 avg60=0.02 avg300=0.01; full avg10=0.00 avg60=0.00 avg300=0.00.
Recovery reasoning: Zero pressure stalls — no task ever waited on reclaim. The alert keys on the wrong counter; repoint it at MemAvailable so backups stop paging us.
<|call:patch_alert_rule|> {"rule": "HostMemLow", "expr": "node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes / node_memory_MemTotal_bytes < 0.10", "for": "10m"}
System Response: rule HostMemLow updated and reloaded; not firing; incident auto-resolved 03:12.
Resolution: No OOM was imminent — the backup filled page cache, MemFree fell to 1.2G while MemAvailable held 42G, and the alert measured MemFree. Rule now fires on MemAvailable < 10% for 10m; PSI full=0.00 confirms zero real pressure; reboot cancelled.

Reasoning: Exit 137 is SIGKILL. Before blaming kubelet, check the node — if it is unpressured, eviction is unlikely and the killer is per-cgroup.
<|call:free_stats|> {"host": "k8s-node-14"}
System Response: Mem: total 128654M used 61988M free 60102M shared 402M buff/cache 6564M available 63911M.
Recovery reasoning: 63.9G available rules out node OOM and kubelet eviction — but that is the wrong layer: the kernel enforces memory.max per-cgroup and will SIGKILL inside one while the host idles. Check dmesg for a memcg kill.
<|call:dmesg_grep|> {"host": "k8s-node-14", "pattern": "Memory cgroup out of memory", "since": "6h"}
System Response: 09:12:41 Memory cgroup out of memory: Killed process 21437 (payments-api) anon-rss:4092412kB — cgroup /kubepods.slice/kubepods-burstable-pod9f2e.slice/cri-containerd-8a13.scope; 3 identical events today.
Recovery reasoning: memcg kills confirmed at anon-rss 4.0G each time. Read the cgroup limit and counters to size the gap.
<|call:cgroup_read|> {"host": "k8s-node-14", "cgroup": "kubepods-burstable-pod9f2e.slice/cri-containerd-8a13.scope", "files": ["memory.max", "memory.peak", "memory.events"]}
System Response: memory.max 4294967296; memory.peak 4294963200; memory.events: low 0 high 0 max 3812 oom 3 oom_kill 3.
Recovery reasoning: Limit 4GiB, peak 4kB under it, 3812 throttle-at-max events before each kill — the working set legitimately reaches 4G. Undersized limit, not a leak. Raise it with headroom.
<|call:kubectl_patch_resources|> {"deployment": "payments-api", "container": "payments-api", "memory_request": "6Gi", "memory_limit": "6Gi"}
System Response: deployment.apps/payments-api patched; rollout 2/2 complete; new cgroup memory.max 6442450944.
Resolution: The kernel memcg OOM killer, not kubelet: memory.max=4Gi sat below the real ~4G working set, so exit 137 fired despite 60G free on the host. At 6Gi a 4h soak shows memory.peak 4.31G, oom 0; restarts stopped, platform bug closed.

Reasoning: MemoryMax judges whole-process RSS while -Xmx bounds only the Java heap, so both claims can be true at once. Get the kernel-side RSS composition first.
<|call:smaps_rollup|> {"host": "quote-prod-2", "pid": 31427}
System Response: Rss 5872640 kB, Anonymous 5701632 kB, Shared_Clean 148992 kB, Swap 0 kB; anon grew 210 MB since 06:00.
Recovery reasoning: 5.6G resident and rising. Now check the heap itself — if the devs are right it is at or under 4G.
<|call:jcmd_gc_heap_info|> {"host": "quote-prod-2", "pid": 31427}
System Response: garbage-first heap: total 4194304K, used 2913280K; GC pauses nominal, last full GC 14h ago. Heap healthy.
Recovery reasoning: Heap healthy at 2.8G under a 4G cap — exactly why this looks unexplainable if heap is all you watch: it covers at most 4G of a 5.6G RSS; the missing ~1.7G is off-heap, invisible to heap dumps. Native Memory Tracking splits RSS by region.
<|call:jcmd_native_memory|> {"host": "quote-prod-2", "pid": 31427, "scope": "summary"}
System Response: Total committed 6762496K: Java Heap 4194304K, Thread 1650688K (1612 threads), Metaspace 204800K, Code 122880K, GC 65536K, Internal+Other 524288K (DirectByteBuffer 498M).
Recovery reasoning: There it is: 1612 threads at the default 1M stack commit 1.6G, plus 498M direct buffers — 4G heap + 1.6G threads + 0.5G buffers + 0.4G metaspace/code/GC tracks the 5.9G kill point. The async-http pool is unbounded; cap it and shrink stacks instead of raising MemoryMax.
<|call:patch_java_opts|> {"service": "quote-engine", "opts": "-Xmx3584m -Xss512k -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize=512m -Dhttp.pool.maxThreads=200", "restart": true}
System Response: jvm.options updated; quote-engine restarted; post-warmup RSS 4188M, threads 214.
Resolution: RSS = heap + thread stacks + direct buffers + metaspace, not just -Xmx: 1612 stacks (1.6G) plus 498M DirectByteBuffers pushed RSS to MemoryMax=6G. With 200 threads, 512k stacks and a 3.5G heap, RSS holds at 4.2G over 48h; no kills, no 12G grant.

Reasoning: Leak-free but ever-growing RSS in a heavily threaded C++ service points at allocator behavior rather than lost objects. Start with the process shape.
<|call:ps_rss_top|> {"host": "ingest-prod-5", "limit": 3}
System Response: ingestd pid 4412: RSS 6912M, VSZ 21340M; next largest process 310M.
Recovery reasoning: 21G virtual against 6.9G resident is arena-shaped, but confirm the live-object story on prod before blaming the allocator.
<|call:heap_leak_check|> {"host": "ingest-prod-5", "pid": 4412, "mode": "lsan-attach"}
System Response: LeakSanitizer: definitely lost 0 bytes, indirectly lost 0 bytes; live allocations 318 MB across 1.2M objects. No leak detected.
Recovery reasoning: The verdict is true at the object layer, useless at the page layer — 318M live versus 6.9G resident means ~6.6G sits in freed-but-retained allocator pages, which no leak checker counts. glibc gives contending threads their own arenas (up to 8 x cores) and frees fragment across those 64MB heaps without returning to the kernel. Verify the map signature.
<|call:pmap_inspect|> {"host": "ingest-prod-5", "pid": 4412, "group_by": "size"}
System Response: 104 anon regions of 65536K (glibc arena heaps): 6.5G reserved, 6.2G resident; main heap 480M; 64 thread stacks 8192K each.
Recovery reasoning: 104 arena heaps on a 16-core box (cap 8 x 16 = 128) is contention-driven arena sprawl, which explains the RSS. Cap arenas to 2; the extra malloc lock contention is fine at our allocation rate.
<|call:patch_unit_env|> {"unit": "ingestd.service", "env": {"MALLOC_ARENA_MAX": "2"}, "restart": true}
System Response: drop-in 10-malloc.conf written; ingestd restarted 14:22; RSS 1.9G after warmup.
Resolution: glibc per-thread malloc arenas — 104 x 64MB heaps holding freed fragments — inflated RSS to 6.9G with only 318M live, which is why ASan and valgrind were clean. With MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2, RSS holds at 2.3G over 72h; no restart cron.

Reasoning: A fragmentation ratio near 2 usually gets blamed on the allocator, but growth tied to BGSAVE hints at fork copy-on-write. Pull Redis's memory view first.
<|call:redis_info_memory|> {"host": "cache-prod-1", "port": 6379}
System Response: used_memory 9.52G, used_memory_rss 19.21G, mem_fragmentation_ratio 2.02 (allocator jemalloc-5.3), latest_fork_usec 812345.
Recovery reasoning: The ratio formally indicts jemalloc fragmentation. Before acting on that, read the allocator's own books — fragmentation it owns would show as resident far above allocated.
<|call:jemalloc_stats|> {"host": "cache-prod-1", "pid": 2201}
System Response: allocated 9.52G, active 9.87G, resident 10.41G, retained 1.2G; allocator overhead 9.3%.
Recovery reasoning: This acquits the subsystem the ratio accused: jemalloc holds only 10.4G resident, so the other 8.8G of RSS lives outside the allocator — kernel-level page inflation. The 812ms fork points at transparent hugepages: with THP always, each 4k write the parent makes during BGSAVE copy-on-writes an entire 2M page. Check THP.
<|call:check_thp_status|> {"host": "cache-prod-1", "pid": 2201}
System Response: /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled: [always] madvise never. smaps_rollup pid 2201: AnonHugePages 17.9G of Rss 19.2G. Redis log: WARNING THP set to always.
Recovery reasoning: 17.9G of anon THP confirms it. Switch to madvise — Redis does not madvise its heap, so it returns to 4k pages — and bounce Redis off-peak.
<|call:set_thp_mode|> {"host": "cache-prod-1", "mode": "madvise", "persist": "tmpfiles.d", "restart_service": "redis-server"}
System Response: THP now [madvise]; redis-server restarted 04:10, RDB reload 41s.
Resolution: THP=always made the BGSAVE fork copy-on-write at 2M granularity: 9.5G of data cost 19.2G RSS (AnonHugePages 17.9G) and 812ms forks stalled p99; jemalloc was innocent — the ratio blamed the wrong subsystem. After madvise: RSS 10.2G, ratio 1.07, fork 9ms, p99 2.1ms; no RAM doubling.

Reasoning: A kill at a fixed job time with tiny process RSS means the charge is likely not process anon memory. Check what the processes hold.
<|call:ps_rss_top|> {"scope": "pod/report-builder", "limit": 5}
System Response: report-builder 912M, fluentbit-sidecar 38M, tini 4M; total RSS 954M of 4096M limit, 3.1G headroom.
Recovery reasoning: 954M cannot trip a 4Gi limit — but a cgroup is billed beyond process RSS: page cache, kernel memory and tmpfs pages, owned by no process. Read the cgroup ledger.
<|call:cgroup_read|> {"cgroup": "kubepods-burstable-pod3c71.slice/cri-containerd-e42b.scope", "files": ["memory.current", "memory.max", "memory.stat", "memory.events"]}
System Response: memory.current 4204789760; memory.max 4294967296; memory.stat: anon 968M, file 2984M (shmem 2941M), kernel 58M; memory.events: oom_kill 6, last 02:10:14.
Recovery reasoning: anon 968M + file 2984M + kernel 58M = 4010M of 4096M, and 2941M of file is shmem — tmpfs pages charged to the writing cgroup yet invisible to ps. Find the memory-backed mount.
<|call:du_path|> {"scope": "pod/report-builder", "path": "/scratch", "flags": "--max-depth=1 -h"}
System Response: /scratch (emptyDir medium=Memory, tmpfs 4.0G): 2.9G — report-20260702.arrow 1.1G, report-20260701.arrow 1.0G, tmp-join-8812.arrow 0.8G; persists across runs.
Recovery reasoning: Arrow spill files accumulate on a memory-backed emptyDir; each 02:10 run tips the charge past 4Gi. The job tolerates disk IO; move scratch off RAM.
<|call:kubectl_patch_volume|> {"deployment": "report-builder", "volume": "scratch", "patch": {"emptyDir": {"medium": "", "sizeLimit": "8Gi"}}}
System Response: deployment patched, pod recreated; /scratch now disk-backed; memory.current 1.02G.
Resolution: tmpfs pages count against the writer's cgroup though ps shows none: 2.9G of Arrow scratch plus 968M anon crossed memory.max=4Gi nightly. Disk-backed emptyDir: next two runs peaked at 1.4G, no new oom_kill; not a kubelet bug.

Reasoning: fork should share the dict copy-on-write; 2.9G workers mean inherited pages are un-sharing. Check headroom first.
<|call:free_stats|> {"host": "etl-prod-2"}
System Response: Mem: total 32011M used 30498M free 512M buff/cache 1001M available 809M; Swap: total 0M.
Recovery reasoning: 809M left, no swap. Get the per-process shape.
<|call:ps_rss_top|> {"host": "etl-prod-2", "filter": "etl", "limit": 10}
System Response: parent 6244M; 8 workers 2870-2955M, +14M/min each; 118M right after fork.
Recovery reasoning: Workers privatize steadily from a shared start. What does the devs' instrumentation see?
<|call:tracemalloc_report|> {"host": "etl-prod-2", "pid": 7714}
System Response: current 41.2MB, peak 55.8MB across 312k blocks; top site batch_decode() 18MB; flat over the last 10min.
Recovery reasoning: Flat tracemalloc against +14M/min RSS is the tell: it only counts Python-allocator allocations after tracing starts; inherited pages are outside its ledger. This is COW un-sharing — CPython writes ob_refcnt on every access, so merely reading the dict dirties inherited pages.
<|call:smaps_rollup|> {"host": "etl-prod-2", "pid": 7714, "compare_minutes": 10}
System Response: Private_Dirty 2841M (+186M/10min), Shared_Clean 3298M (-181M/10min); private grows as shared shrinks, byte-for-byte.
Recovery reasoning: Textbook COW un-share, times eight. Put features behind a mapping the refcounter never writes; freeze GC before fork.
<|call:patch_worker_config|> {"service": "etl-runner", "change": "features to Arrow file; workers open via pyarrow.memory_map; gc.freeze() before fork", "deploy": "rolling"}
System Response: r2026.07.03-1 deployed; parent 1.9G, workers 176-201M after 90min.
Resolution: fork COW inflation: refcount writes dirtied inherited pages on mere reads, un-sharing ~2.8G per worker, while tracemalloc read 41MB because inherited pages bypass the Python allocator. mmap-backed features plus gc.freeze(): host peaks 10.4G, kills stopped.

Reasoning: Latency up with green CPU and no kills often means reclaim stalls, invisible to utilization panels. Scan the dashboard.
<|call:check_host_dashboard|> {"host": "gw-prod-7", "panels": ["cpu", "memory", "disk"]}
System Response: [GREEN] cpu 31% avg (cpu9 at 96%), memory used 91% available 2.8G, disk util 44%, iowait 2.1%.
Recovery reasoning: The green hides that pinned core: one core buried on a 31%-average box is a kernel-thread signature — kswapd is single-threaded per node. Utilization cannot show memory stalls; PSI can.
<|call:psi_read|> {"host": "gw-prod-7", "resource": "memory"}
System Response: some avg10=38.2 avg60=34.7 avg300=29.1; full avg10=11.4 avg60=9.8.
Recovery reasoning: Tasks wait on memory 38% of wall time; whole-host stalls 11% — severe pressure despite 2.8G available. Is reclaim churning without freeing?
<|call:vmstat_read|> {"host": "gw-prod-7", "fields": ["pgscan_kswapd", "pgsteal_kswapd", "workingset_refault_file"], "interval": "60s"}
System Response: pgscan_kswapd 1.94M/min, pgsteal_kswapd 82k/min (4.2% efficiency), workingset_refault_file 241k/min.
Recovery reasoning: 1.9M scanned to steal 82k, plus 241k refaults/min: the working set is evicted and re-read — the gateway's mmap'd routing data keeps refaulting. Who squeezed the cache?
<|call:ps_rss_top|> {"host": "gw-prod-7", "limit": 3}
System Response: log-indexer 24312M (started yesterday 17:40), api-gateway 3121M, envoy 411M.
Recovery reasoning: The indexer's 24G of anon leaves ~4G for everything else. Cap it; its batch SLO tolerates throttling.
<|call:systemd_set_property|> {"host": "gw-prod-7", "unit": "log-indexer.service", "properties": {"MemoryHigh": "12G", "MemoryMax": "14G"}, "persist": true}
System Response: applied; log-indexer reclaimed to 12.1G in 8min; kswapd cpu 3%.
Resolution: pressure invisible to utilization: PSI some=38%/full=11%, kswapd at 4% reclaim efficiency, 241k refaults/min after the 24G squeeze. MemoryHigh=12G: PSI full 0.0, refaults 1.8k/min, p99 43ms.

Reasoning: 59G used minus 6.1G process RSS leaves ~53G in kernel/cache; reboot-fixes-it means accumulating kernel objects. Confirm real pressure.
<|call:psi_read|> {"host": "ci-runner-9", "resource": "memory"}
System Response: some avg10=22.6 avg60=19.4; full avg10=6.2 avg60=5.5.
Recovery reasoning: Tasks wait on memory 22% of the time — the slowdown is real. Where do the bytes sit?
<|call:read_meminfo|> {"host": "ci-runner-9", "fields": ["MemTotal", "MemAvailable", "Slab", "SReclaimable", "SUnreclaim"]}
System Response: MemTotal 65821440 kB, MemAvailable 41250816 kB, Slab 36782080 kB, SReclaimable 35946496 kB, SUnreclaim 835584 kB. Status OK (reclaimable slab counts as available).
